That looks suspicious to me.  I haven't used Windows in a long time, but 
when I did, that directory would have been write protected.  When I 
would try to install a package there it would fail, and R would add a 
user library and install there instead.  The .libPaths() vector would be 
changed for the rest of that session, but could revert to just one entry 
if I started a new session.

> 
> But maybe the post by Jinsong ZZhao is relevent here:

Yes, that could explain everything.  If the date when a binary package 
is built changes, that could mean that different C libraries were linked 
into it, and you should use the newer one.  Or it might just mean that 
CRAN rebuilt it unnecessarily, but there's no way for R to know that.

